Overview
In the previous lab, Build Enterprise Agents, you built an Code Assist Agent. Two of its specialist agents had their tool credentials baked in: the github_agent on Agent Runtime read a GitHub personal access token from its .env, and the salesforce_agent on Cloud Run read a Salesforce Connected App consumer key and secret the same way.

In this lab, you remove both hardcoded secrets and replace them with Agent Identity Auth Manager. The Auth Manager is a Google-managed credentials vault: you store a credential once, and your agent reads it at tool-call time using its own identity, never a secret embedded in code or an environment variable. You configure two kinds of auth provider: an API key provider for the GitHub MCP server and a 2-legged OAuth (2LO) provider for Salesforce. You will refactor each agent to resolve its credential through the ADK, redeploy, and grant each agent's identity the permission to read the credentials.

By the end, neither agent contains a tool secret, and you can rotate either credential in one place without touching or redeploying the agent.

Hardcoded secrets to reading credentials from Auth Manager auth providers

Objectives
In this lab, you learn how to perform the following tasks:

Create auth providers for API-key and 2-legged OAuth auth providers in Agent Identity Auth Manager.
Refactor ADK agents to resolve tool credentials through the auth manager instead of hardcoded secrets.
Enable Agent Identity on an Agent Runtime agent and grant its SPIFFE principal access to an auth provider.
Grant a Cloud Run agent's service account access to an auth provider, and verify both agents end to end.
Prerequisites
This builds on the ideas explored in Build Enterprise Agents.

Important: Save your third-party credentials in a persistent local location.

This lab interacts with external third-party platforms. If you plan to complete the related labs (Build Enterprise Agents and Observe and Secure a Multi-Agent System), you will reuse the same third-party credentials across those sessions.

Save the following credentials:

GitHub: Personal Access Token (classic) with repo and read:org scopes
Salesforce: Connected App Consumer Key (Client ID)
Salesforce: Connected App Consumer Secret (Client Secret)
Salesforce: My Domain URL
You should be comfortable with Python, gcloud, and ADK.

