Agent Identity and the Auth Manager
A hardcoded credential is a liability. A token in a .env file is copied into the deployment, visible to anyone who can read the config, and rotated only by editing and redeploying every agent that uses it. Agent Identity Auth Manager removes the secret from that path entirely.

Two ideas make this work, and it is worth separating them:

Agent Identity is who the agent is. Each deployed agent can be given a strong, Google-managed identity based on the SPIFFE standard. On Agent Runtime this is a dedicated SPIFFE principal tied to the agent's resource; on Cloud Run the agent acts as its service account. Either way, the agent authenticates as itself, not as a shared secret.
Auth Manager is what the agent is allowed to read. You store a credential (an API key, or an OAuth client id and secret) in an auth provider. Access to each auth provider is governed by IAM, so only the identities you authorize can retrieve it.
At runtime the pieces meet inside the ADK. When your agent calls an external tool, the ADK asks the Auth Manager for the auth provider credential, the Auth Manager returns it to the agent, and the ADK injects it into the outbound request as the right header (for example, Authorization). Your code never sees the raw secret, and the auth manager refreshes OAuth tokens for you.

Note: Agent Identity Auth Manager is a Preview feature. The gcloud commands in this lab use the alpha component, which is installed in Cloud Shell by default.
Two runtimes, two identities
Your two agents live on different runtimes, so they present different identities to the auth manager. This is the single most important idea in the lab, because it changes one concrete step: who you grant access to.

Agent Runtime (GitHub agent). You enable Agent Identity at deploy time, and the platform assigns the agent a SPIFFE principal that looks like principal://agents.global.org-ORGANIZATION_ID.system.id.goog/resources/aiplatform/projects/PROJECT_NUMBER/locations/LOCATION/reasoningEngines/ENGINE_ID. You grant that principal access to the auth provider.
Cloud Run (Salesforce agent). The agent authenticates with its Cloud Run service account through Application Default Credentials. You grant that service account access to the auth provider.
The role you grant is the same in both cases: Agent Identity User (roles/agentidentity.user) on the auth provider, but the member string differs.

Agent Runtime agent uses a SPIFFE principal and Cloud Run agent uses a service account, both granted on auth providers

