Task 3. Move the GitHub agent onto the auth provider
In this task, you replace the GitHub agent's starter code with a version already refactored to read its token from the auth provider, deploy it to Agent Runtime with Agent Identity enabled, and grant the agent's SPIFFE principal permission to use the auth provider.

Note: This task deploys the GitHub agent with a small shell script, deploy_with_identity.sh, instead of agents-cli deploy --agent-identity. As of the current agents-cli/adk releases, that flag combines identity provisioning and code deployment into a single API request that Vertex AI Agent Runtime rejects — every such deploy fails with "failed to start and cannot serve traffic." deploy_with_identity.sh performs the same two steps agents-cli does (provision the agent's identity, then deploy the code) as two separate requests, which the backend accepts. You still use agents-cli for the Salesforce agent later in this lab — this issue is specific to Agent Runtime with Agent Identity.
Review how the agent authenticates today
In the Cloud Shell Editor, open ~/agents/github-agent/agent/agent.py.
Notice that the toolset hardcodes the token into an Authorization header built from GITHUB_TOKEN. The secret travels with the deployment. You replace this whole project with an Agent-Identity-ready version that resolves the credential through the auth manager at call time instead.
Replace the agent's starter code
Replace the entire content of ~/agents/github-agent/agent/agent.py with the following code. The changes from what you saw a moment ago are focused: the token read is gone, the ADK's GcpAuthProvider is registered, the toolset is given a GcpAuthProviderScheme, and a header_provider maps the resolved credential into the Authorization header.

You will complete the TODO items in the code in the following steps.

import os

from dotenv import load_dotenv
from google.adk.agents import Agent
from google.adk.apps import App
from google.adk.auth.credential_manager import CredentialManager
from google.adk.integrations.agent_identity import GcpAuthProvider, GcpAuthProviderScheme
from google.adk.models import Gemini
from google.adk.tools.mcp_tool import McpToolset, StreamableHTTPConnectionParams
from google.genai import types

load_dotenv()

MODEL = os.environ["MODEL"]
GITHUB_MCP_URL = os.getenv("GITHUB_MCP_URL", "https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/")
# Full resource name of the API-key auth provider you created in Task 2.
GITHUB_AUTH_PROVIDER_URI = os.environ["GITHUB_AUTH_PROVIDER_URI"]

# Only expose the read tools this agent needs.
GITHUB_TOOL_FILTER = ["search_repositories", "search_issues", "list_issues"]

# TODO 1: register the Google Cloud auth provider so the
# CredentialManager can resolve the auth provider credential using
# the agent's own identity. This runs once at import.
raise NotImplementedError("Register the auth provider")

# TODO 2: create the auth scheme that names which auth provider the ADK
# should resolve before the tool runs, e.g.
#     github_auth_scheme = GcpAuthProviderScheme(name=GITHUB_AUTH_PROVIDER_URI)
raise NotImplementedError("Create github_auth_scheme")


def github_mcp_toolset() -> McpToolset:
    """MCP toolset for the GitHub server, authenticated via auth manager."""
    toolset = None

    def github_header_provider(readonly_ctx) -> dict[str, str]:
        if not toolset or not toolset.get_auth_config():
            return {}
        auth_config = toolset.get_auth_config()
        cred = None
        if readonly_ctx and hasattr(readonly_ctx, "get_credential"):
            cred = readonly_ctx.get_credential(auth_config.credential_key)
        if not cred:
            cred = auth_config.exchanged_auth_credential
        if not cred or not cred.http:
            return {}
        token = None
        if cred.http.credentials and cred.http.credentials.token:
            token = cred.http.credentials.token
        elif cred.http.additional_headers:
            token = cred.http.additional_headers.get(
                "X-API-Key"
            ) or cred.http.additional_headers.get("X-GOOG-API-KEY")
        if token:
            return {"Authorization": f"Bearer {token}"}
        return {}

    # TODO 3: Build and return the McpToolset as github_mcp_toolset.
    # Pass the connection params 
    # (StreamableHTTPConnectionParams(url=GITHUB_MCP_URL)),
    # the github_auth_scheme, the GITHUB_TOOL_FILTER, and the
    # header_provider. No PAT / hardcoded Authorization header.
    raise NotImplementedError("Build and return the McpToolset")


root_agent = Agent(
    name="github_agent",
    model=Gemini(
        model=MODEL,
        retry_options=types.HttpRetryOptions(attempts=3),
    ),
    description=(
        "Specialized assistant for GitHub: searches repositories, issues, and "
        "pull requests via the GitHub MCP server."
    ),
    instruction=(
        "You are a specialized assistant for interacting with GitHub. "
        "Use the provided tools to search for repositories, find issues, and "
        "retrieve pull-request information, then answer with what you find. "
        "Be concise and cite the repository names / issue numbers you used."
    ),
    tools=[github_mcp_toolset()],
)

app = App(root_agent=root_agent, name="app")
Copied!
Following this API key example in the Agent Identity auth manager documentation, complete TODO 1 in the code you pasted to register the Google Cloud auth provider to resolve the auth provider credential.

Complete TODO 2 in the code to create the auth scheme using GcpAuthProviderScheme using the URI of the auth provider you created in the previous task. That auth provider URI should now be saved in the GITHUB_AUTH_PROVIDER_URI variable.

Complete TODO 3 in the code you pasted above to build and return an McpToolset. It should use StreamableHTTPConnectionParams pointed to your GITHUB_MCP_URL. It should use the auth_scheme you defined in the previous step and a tool_filter set to GITHUB_TOOL_FILTER . Set the header_provider parameter to github_header_provider.

In the Cloud Shell Editor, open ~/agents/github-agent/agent/main.py. This is what serves the agent over the A2A protocol.

Note: Rather than agents-cli's usual generated FastAPI app, or the native ADK entrypoint (adk api_server --a2a), this project builds its A2A agent card dynamically at startup with ADK's AgentCardBuilder, the same mechanism agents-cli uses internally. This sidesteps a current ADK bug where adk api_server --a2a's static-agent-card loading path (fast_api.py) raises an UnboundLocalError and never registers any A2A route. main.py is otherwise a small, plain FastAPI app with no other scaffolding.
Understand how the token reaches the tool
In this case, the GitHub agent talks to a remote MCP server, which expects the credential to arrive as an HTTP header on every tool call. So we need to wire the token in three parts:

CredentialManager.register_auth_provider(GcpAuthProvider()) teaches the ADK how to talk to the auth manager. It runs once at import.
GcpAuthProviderScheme(name=GITHUB_AUTH_PROVIDER_URI), passed to the McpToolset as auth_scheme, tells the ADK which auth provider to resolve before the tool runs. The ADK exchanges the agent's identity for the stored API key and holds it in the toolset's auth_config.
header_provider=github_header_provider is the bridge. Just before each MCP request the ADK calls this function; you read the resolved credential out of the auth_config and return {"Authorization": "Bearer <token>"}. The MCP server receives an ordinary bearer token, and the raw secret never appears in your code or configuration.
The key idea: for an MCP tool you cannot receive the credential as a function argument, so you intercept the outgoing request with a header_provider and attach the token yourself.

Deploy with Agent Identity enabled
Run the deploy script. This assigns the agent a SPIFFE principal that it uses to read the auth provider.

cd ~/agents/github-agent
chmod 777 ./deploy_with_identity.sh
REGION=REGION A2A_APP_NAME=agent AUTH_PROVIDER_NAME=github-mcp-auth-provider DISPLAY_NAME=github-agent-v2 ./deploy_with_identity.sh
Copied!
This takes 5-10 minutes and prints its progress as it goes, so there is no separate status-polling command to run. Under the hood it does exactly what agents-cli deploy --agent-identity is supposed to do, just as two separate API calls instead of one: it first creates the Reasoning Engine with only its identity set, letting the platform cleanly provision the SPIFFE principal; grants that principal the baseline roles it needs to run; and only then deploys your code, in an update that never touches the identity again. When it finishes it prints the Effective identity and Reasoning Engine ID you need next.

Note: If the deployment fails or crashes, re-running deploy_with_identity.sh will reuse the existing reasoning engine associated with github-agent-v2. To retry with a clean deployment, either delete the failed reasoning engine in the Google Cloud console or specify a new display name in the deploy command (for example, DISPLAY_NAME=github-agent-v3).
Copy the Effective identity value from the script's output and set it as a variable, prefixed with principal://.

export GITHUB_AGENT_IDENTITY="principal://PASTE_THE_EFFECTIVE_IDENTITY_HERE"
Copied!
Note: The identity has the form agents.global.org-ORGANIZATION_ID.system.id.goog/resources/aiplatform/projects/PROJECT_NUMBER/locations/REGION/reasoningEngines/ENGINE_ID — the script prints it bare; the principal:// prefix above is what IAM binding commands expect.
Copy the Reasoning Engine ID value from the script's output and build the agent's URL.

export GITHUB_ENGINE_ID="PASTE_THE_REASONING_ENGINE_ID_HERE"
export GITHUB_AGENT_URL="https://${REGION}-aiplatform.googleapis.com/v1/projects/${PROJECT_NUMBER}/locations/${REGION}/reasoningEngines/${GITHUB_ENGINE_ID}"
echo ${GITHUB_AGENT_URL}
