When the deployment finishes, agents-cli prints the Cloud Run service URL and the service account the service runs as. Note the difference from Task 3: there is no --agent-identity flag and no SPIFFE principal. A Cloud Run service authenticates to Google Cloud as its service account, so that service account is the identity you authorize next.

Grant the service account access to the auth provider
Unlike the GitHub agent, the Salesforce agent runs on Cloud Run, so it reads the auth provider as its service account, not as a SPIFFE principal.

Read the service account the Cloud Run service runs as.

export SF_SA=$(gcloud run services describe salesforce-agent --region ${REGION} \
  --format='value(spec.template.spec.serviceAccountName)')
echo ${SF_SA}
Copied!
Grant that service account the Agent Identity User (roles/agentidentity.user) role on the Salesforce auth provider (salesforce-2lo-auth-provider) by constructing a gcloud alpha agent-identity auth-providers add-iam-policy-binding command. The member should begin with a prefix of serviceAccount: followed by the value of ${SF_SA} which you set earlier.

Note: If the agent cannot reach the auth manager after this grant, confirm the service account also has roles/serviceusage.serviceUsageConsumer on the project, which is required to call the Agent Identity API.
For local development and testing, additionally provide your user identity access to the auth provider. In the below command, replace EMAIL_ADDRESS with this Qwiklab's user email.

gcloud alpha agent-identity auth-providers add-iam-policy-binding salesforce-2lo-auth-provider \
--project=${PROJECT_ID} \
--location=${LOCATION} \
--role="roles/agentidentity.user" \
--member="user:EMAIL_ADDRESS"
Copied!
Test the agent
If Identity-Aware Proxy (IAP) is enabled on the service, disable it first. IAP puts a Google sign-in in front of the service, which blocks the identity token that agents-cli run sends, so the call cannot reach the agent.

gcloud run services update salesforce-agent --region=${REGION} --no-iap
